1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,760
Vitamin D deficiency — if you've been wondering whether it's just you, even in the middle of summer, save this before you forget.

2
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:23,200
According to NHANES data, roughly one in four US adults have vitamin D levels too low for bone and overall health, and the rate climbs even higher during fall and winter, among women, and among people who spend most of their time indoors.

3
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,040
Here's the surprising part.

4
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:28,880
Population studies show it's not really sunscreen driving this.

5
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:37,360
Staying in the shade and covering up with long sleeves are linked to lower vitamin D levels more strongly than sunscreen use itself.

6
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:44,960
The bigger driver is simply spending most of the day indoors — desk jobs, commuting by car, air-conditioned offices.

7
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:49,760
Vitamin D isn't just about bones, it's tied to metabolism too.

8
00:00:50,080 --> 00:01:07,760
A UK Biobank study of more than 335,000 people, published in Frontiers in Nutrition in July 2026, found that insulin resistance and vitamin D deficiency together raised cardiovascular risk by 26% more than either factor alone.

9
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:13,680
Researchers now call this combination a high-risk phenotype worth screening for together.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,600
There are four habits that help.

11
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:20,960
Fortified foods, fatty fish, egg yolks, and short daily sun breaks.

12
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,200
First, fortified foods.

13
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:31,760
Milk, orange juice, and cereal in the US are commonly fortified with vitamin D — check the label and make them part of your routine.

14
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,680
Second, fatty fish.

15
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,280
Salmon, mackerel, and tuna are some of the richest natural sources of vitamin D.

16
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,680
Aim for at least two servings a week.

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,520
Third, egg yolks.

18
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,480
Don't stick to egg whites only — the yolk is where the vitamin D is.

19
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,800
Finally, short sun breaks.

20
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:59,200
Stepping outside for just 15 minutes with your arms or legs exposed makes a real difference — skip the peak midday sun if you can.

21
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:07,920
Fortified foods, fatty fish, egg yolks, and short daily sun breaks — these four habits reliably raise vitamin D levels.

22
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:14,880
If your last checkup didn't include a 25-hydroxyvitamin D blood test, ask for one at your next visit.

23
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:23,680
And if you already have insulin resistance or a metabolic condition, don't start high-dose supplements on your own — talk to your doctor first.
